  /* Facts parents ask about */
  var FACTS = [
    { title: 'Two or more languages are fine', text: 'Speaking Liberian English, Kpelle, Bassa, Kru or any other language at home does not cause speech delay. Speak the language you know best, richly and often. Children can learn more than one language.' },
    { title: 'Screens and talking', text: 'WHO advises that children aged 2–4 have no more than 1 hour of sitting screen time a day, and less is better. Talking with a person teaches language; videos do much less. A TV on in the background reduces how much adults talk to children.' },
    { title: 'Mouth exercises', text: 'Blowing, tongue and lip exercises without speech sounds have not been shown to improve talking. Spend the time on play and talking instead.' },
    { title: 'Boys and late talkers', text: 'Some children talk late and catch up, but you cannot know which ones by waiting. Starting home practice early does no harm and helps every child.' },
    { title: 'Attention at 3', text: 'Most 3-year-olds focus on one activity for only a few minutes, and up to 5–10 minutes by age 5 (CDC). Being very active is normal at this age. If attention problems are strong in every place (home, church, school) and continue as your child grows, talk with a doctor.' },
    { title: 'Diets for speech or autism', text: 'Special diets (such as gluten-free and dairy-free) and supplements like fish oil have not been shown to improve talking. A balanced diet supports growth and learning.' }
  ];

